#
# The ANT+ compatibility profiles' policy settings.
# docs/radiant-security.md section 11.6 is the table these mirror.

menu "RadiANT profile policy"

# ── Sourced-ness, which the C has to be able to tell from "set to n" ────────
#
# An application that does not source this file at all - the radiant unit
# test image is one - sees none of these symbols, and the C then has to fall
# back on the DEFAULTS of docs/radiant-security.md section 11.6's table rather
# than on "unset". For a `bool` those two are indistinguishable from the
# preprocessor, so this symbol is the difference: it is defined whenever this
# menu exists, and profile_private.c reads section 11.6's default when it does
# not.
config RADIANT_PROFILE_POLICY
	bool
	default y

config RADIANT_ATTEST_ID
	bool "attest_id: Tier I identity attestation"
	depends on RADIANT_SEC_COMPAT
	default y
	help
	  One self-contained page every T seconds proving "this stream comes
	  from the holder of K_auth, now, and is not a replay". It covers no
	  payload, so it is verifiable on receipt and a packet lost anywhere
	  else costs nothing.

	  ON BY DEFAULT, and it is the most valuable thing in the compat layer:
	  a stranger's sensor on an overlapping device number is the mainstream
	  failure and this answers it cryptographically, at ~1.2% of slots
	  against the 1.65% ANT+ itself already spends on common pages 80 and
	  81. The compatibility cost of authentication is smaller than a
	  mechanism ANT+ ships.

	  Turning it off is a supported posture - a plain ANT+ sensor with
	  nothing added - but note what it costs below: every private_policy
	  except `never`, and the one exemption, depends on it.

choice
	prompt "announce: what a switch says on the air"
	depends on RADIANT_SEC_COMPAT
	default RADIANT_PRIVATE_ANNOUNCE_BROADCAST
	help
	  What the node puts on the air when it leaves the compat channel for
	  the private one, and what it costs.

	  NEITHER OF THESE IS "MORE SECURE". broadcast buys every keyed
	  receiver - including the ones that did not ask - a handover bounded by
	  retune time, and costs unlinkability at the moment of the switch:
	  an observer present learns that compat device number X and private
	  device number Y are one node. silent buys that unlinkability back and
	  pays for it in availability: every listener, including the one that
	  asked, loses the stream for as long as a search takes.

config RADIANT_PRIVATE_ANNOUNCE_BROADCAST
	bool "broadcast - SWITCH/RETURN frames on a countdown (default)"
	help
	  The node announces the switch in the clear, over a K-message
	  countdown, with the beacon promoted to one frame in eight for the
	  duration. Every keyed receiver reads the remaining count and retunes
	  on the same message, so a handover looks like a handover rather than
	  N independent dropouts.

	  The announcement carries its own 48-bit tag under K_auth, so it cannot
	  be forged into "everybody follow me to channel X", and a receiver acts
	  on it only after the tag verifies.

config RADIANT_PRIVATE_ANNOUNCE_SILENT
	bool "silent - nothing on the air; the channel simply closes"
	help
	  No SWITCH frames, no locator field in the beacon, no pending-switch
	  bit and no beacon promotion. Keyholders rederive the private device
	  number - trunc16(CMAC(K_id, "priv" || epoch)) - and re-acquire, so
	  they are not lost, merely slower.

	  This is a legitimate posture and not a degradation. It is also the one
	  setting that removes the last on-air authentication requirement, which
	  is why `physical` + `silent` is the one policy combination permitted
	  with attestation switched off entirely.

endchoice

choice
	prompt "private_policy: whether this node can stop being an ANT+ sensor"
	default RADIANT_PRIVATE_POLICY_NEVER
	help
	  Confidentiality is a SWITCH, not an addition. A ciphertext page beside
	  the plaintext page carrying the same value is theatre, so the only way
	  privacy means anything is if the clear stream stops - and a node whose
	  clear stream has stopped is no longer an ANT+ sensor.

	  WHILE PRIVATE, A GARMIN HEAD UNIT AND ZWIFT SEE NOTHING AT ALL. That
	  is the price of confidentiality and there is no version of it that is
	  cheaper. What is bought is not only privacy: device type 0x60 is also
	  where coded PHY, frequency agility, length extension and bounded
	  uplink latency become available, all of which exist only because the
	  compatibility constraint was left behind.

	  None of this is mandatory and the default is that none of it happens.

config RADIANT_PRIVATE_POLICY_NEVER
	bool "never - a byte-exact ANT+ sensor for its whole life (default)"
	help
	  THE DEFAULT, AND THE SETTING A SHIPPED STRAP SHOULD SHIP IN. The node
	  never leaves the compat channel. It will refuse an authenticated
	  switch command from a keyholder it trusts, and count the refusal.

	  Attestation with policy `never` is a perfectly good configuration and
	  probably the most useful one here: a strap that is byte-exact ANT+ to
	  every receiver in the room and provably itself to the one holding its
	  key.

config RADIANT_PRIVATE_POLICY_PHYSICAL
	bool "physical - switches only on a physical action at the node"
	depends on RADIANT_SEC_COMPAT
	depends on RADIANT_ATTEST_ID || RADIANT_PRIVATE_ANNOUNCE_SILENT
	help
	  A button, a magnet or a strap re-seat. NO OVER-AIR PATH EXISTS AT ALL,
	  which is the right setting for a device with a button and a user who
	  wants privacy on demand.

	  With announce = silent this is the one combination that needs no
	  attestation: nothing is announced, so there is nothing to forge, and
	  no over-air trigger, so there is nothing to authenticate. With
	  announce = broadcast it needs Tier I, because the announcement's tag
	  needs K_auth and Tier I's counter.

config RADIANT_PRIVATE_POLICY_COMMAND
	bool "command - a physical action, or an authenticated command"
	depends on RADIANT_SEC_COMPAT && RADIANT_ATTEST_ID
	help
	  A strict superset of `physical`: the node also accepts a command from
	  a paired keyholder, authenticated under K_cmd with Tier I's counter in
	  the nonce, so a recorded command replayed later fails.

	  An UNAUTHENTICATED over-air request never triggers anything, in any
	  policy. A stranger who could mute your power meter for every legacy
	  receiver in the room would be a one-packet denial of service against
	  everybody, and the sensor cannot switch "just for one receiver" - it
	  has one stream and N listeners.

config RADIANT_PRIVATE_POLICY_ALWAYS
	bool "always - never appears as an ANT+ sensor at all"
	depends on RADIANT_SEC_COMPAT && RADIANT_ATTEST_ID
	help
	  The node boots straight onto device type 0x60 and never enters the
	  compat channel. This is exactly today's radiant_sec node, named as a
	  policy state so the axis is complete and so the strongest privacy
	  posture has somewhere to live.

	  Nothing is announced, because there was never a compat channel with
	  listeners on it to announce to.

endchoice

config RADIANT_PRIVATE_COUNTDOWN
	int "K: the switch countdown, in transmitted messages"
	depends on !RADIANT_PRIVATE_POLICY_NEVER
	default 64
	range 16 128
	help
	  How long the node announces a switch before it happens. 64 messages is
	  ~16 s at 4 Hz and gives a receiver eight promoted beacon slots to hear
	  it in.

	  ONE OF 16, 32, 64 OR 128 - the build fails on anything else, because
	  the countdown field carries six bits of eight-message intervals and
	  both ends have to agree on what a countdown can be.

	  The airtime cost is real - the beacon rises to one frame in eight for
	  the duration, ~12% of slots - and it is bounded to the countdown and
	  paid only at a switch.

config RADIANT_PRIVATE_MAX_S
	int "the bounded private duration, in seconds"
	depends on !RADIANT_PRIVATE_POLICY_NEVER
	default 3600
	range 60 86400
	help
	  A SENSOR THAT SILENTLY STAYS PRIVATE IS INDISTINGUISHABLE FROM A DEAD
	  ONE, so private mode is bounded and self-reverting. One hour by
	  default: long enough for the ride the feature exists for, short enough
	  that a node whose owner walked away is an ANT+ sensor again before
	  they wonder whether it is broken.

	  The other revert paths need no setting: a power cycle (the state is
	  never persisted), an authenticated command, and a physical action.

choice
	prompt "enrol: how a new receiver gets this node's key"
	default RADIANT_ENROL_PHYSICAL
	help
	  Adding a receiver to an existing encrypted network. A user buys a
	  second head unit in March and has to be able to add it to a strap they
	  set up in January, without re-provisioning the first head unit and
	  without taking the strap apart.

	  Read this before choosing, because it is the whole trade: PAIRING
	  HAPPENS IN THE CLEAR. An active attacker present during the window can
	  be the man in the middle. The mitigation is a six-digit fingerprint a
	  human compares ACROSS TWO SCREENS - and a strap has no screen, so over
	  the air that comparison is one-sided. Out-of-band provisioning, a
	  printed key or a QR code, genuinely defeats the attack rather than
	  mitigating it, and it stays the recommended path for anything that
	  matters.

	  And the asymmetry nobody expects: RadiANT v1 can ADD a receiver in
	  sixty seconds and can REMOVE one only by re-provisioning the whole
	  network, because every keyholder holds the same root key. Per-receiver
	  revocation is out of scope. Read that sentence before choosing
	  open-window.

config RADIANT_ENROL_CLOSED
	bool "closed - no over-air enrolment, ever"
	help
	  Keys arrive only out of band: the host message set on a host-attached
	  node, or the manufacturer's provisioning path. There is no over-air
	  pairing window and no way to open one.

	  THIS IS THE RECOMMENDED SETTING FOR ANYTHING THAT MATTERS. No
	  protocol, no attack surface during pairing at all, and no dependence
	  on the user actually looking at a number. It is not the default only
	  because a sealed strap with no port has no path at all under it.

config RADIANT_ENROL_PHYSICAL
	bool "physical - a bounded window opened by a physical action (default)"
	help
	  A 60-second pairing window opened by a button, a magnet or a strap
	  re-seat at the node. One pairing per window, and a second press does
	  not extend the first.

	  The window opens on whichever channel the node is currently on - the
	  frames are additional pages, not a channel change - so every receiver
	  that is already listening keeps its stream throughout, and a node in
	  private mode does not have to leave it to gain a receiver.

	  The mitigations are the bounded window, one pairing per window, the
	  physical trigger, and optionally reduced transmit power so the
	  attacker has to be in the room. They are mitigations, not a fix: the
	  fingerprint is still one-sided on a device with no display.

config RADIANT_ENROL_OPEN_WINDOW
	bool "open-window - a keyholder may open a window with a command"
	help
	  A window a CURRENT KEYHOLDER opens with an authenticated command, for
	  a node with no button and no host. Rate-limited to one window every
	  five minutes and bounded to sixty seconds.

	  THIS IS THE WEAKEST OF THE THREE and it exists because a sealed strap
	  otherwise has no path at all. The authentication of the command is the
	  command layer's, not this module's: a node that opens a window on an
	  unverified command has built "anybody may enrol against me".

	  Combined with the paragraph above about removal - add in sixty
	  seconds, remove only by re-provisioning - this is the setting that
	  most deserves a second reading before a product ships with it.

endchoice

endmenu
